  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM EST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nStill hanging onto some 925-850mb moisture this afternoon, mainly \nalong and E of I-65. This is being in enhanced a bit by a fetch from \nLake Michigan, however only sensible weather impacts are a broken \nstratocu deck. Surface high pressure should continue to build into \nthe area tonight, and should slowly help to eradicate the remaining \nclouds. Another good night of radiational cooling conditions, so \nexpect river valley fog to develop once again. Could be dense in \nspots. Overnight lows look to drop into the upper 20s and low 30s \nacross the area.\n\nPleasant Late Fall Week Ahead...\n\nThe upper pattern will feature a gradually increasing upper ridge \nover our area through early week, peaking/centering over our region \nby mid week, and then gradually letting go over the region through \nlate week into the weekend. Even though it will be letting go by \nlate week/weekend, any notable weather holds off until the 2nd half \nof the weekend. At the surface, anomalously strong high pressure \n(98th percentile for this time of year) will also be in control this \nweek, and combined with the upper ridging, yields very confidence in \na dry sensible weather.\n\nGiven the prolonged period of dry conditions combined with \nincreasing thicknesses thanks to the riding, temperatures are \nexpected to warm each successive day, and highs are expected to be \nin the low and mid 60s by mid week, and mid to upper 60s by late \nweek. These values will be well above normal for this time of year, \ngenerally 10 to 15 degrees above normal. We could be in the low 70s \nfor some spots by Saturday, and this will be closer to the full 15 \ndegrees above normal. Despite the mild afternoons, overnight lows \nwill still be quite cold in the upper 20s and low 30s through mid \nweek. Then, 30s and 40s for late week as light southerly flow takes \nback hold of the region.\n\nSunday...\n\nThe next chance for rain doesn't come back into the picture until \nSunday, and even that potential seems to be trending downward. The \n15/12z suite of models suggests that system approaching from \nupstream will be more moisture starved than previous runs, and much \nof the precipitation potential will be gobbled up by the antecedent \ndry airmass. Pops for Sunday have been lowered to the 20-30% range \nreflecting the latest trend downward.",
